---
<Context title="Using the UBS apparatus in Paratext" label="Guide" lastChecked="2026-11-20" sources={sources}
  description="The UBS apparatus lives in the Paratext Source Language Text window. How to open it beside your translation, read its marks and decode the sigla.">
  <p class="lead">In Paratext the UBS apparatus lives in the <em>Source Language Text</em> window, which shows the Hebrew or Greek text beside your draft. A small red number after a Greek word marks an apparatus entry. Click it and the entry pops up.</p>

  <figure class="ptx-fig">
    <div class="ptx-scroll">
      <svg class="ptx-layout" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="ptx-lt">
        <title id="ptx-lt">Sketch of a Paratext screen: your project on the left, the Source Language Text in the middle with a popup opening from a red number, and a Text Collection on the right.</title>
        <rect x="1" y="1" width="638" height="298" rx="10" class="frame"/>
        <text x="16" y="20" class="lbl">Paratext</text>
        <rect x="14" y="32" width="190" height="254" rx="6" class="pane"/>
        <text x="26" y="52" class="h">Your project</text>
        <rect x="26" y="68" width="160" height="6" class="ln"/><rect x="26" y="82" width="140" height="6" class="ln"/><rect x="26" y="96" width="155" height="6" class="ln"/><rect x="26" y="110" width="120" height="6" class="ln"/>
        <rect x="220" y="32" width="200" height="254" rx="6" class="pane"/>
        <text x="232" y="52" class="h">Source Language Text</text>
        <rect x="232" y="68" width="170" height="6" class="ln"/><rect x="232" y="82" width="80" height="6" class="ln"/>
        <circle cx="320" cy="80" r="5" class="mark"/>
        <rect x="232" y="96" width="160" height="6" class="ln"/>
        <path d="M320 86 L300 136" class="arrow"/>
        <rect x="232" y="136" width="178" height="80" rx="5" class="pop"/>
        <text x="242" y="156" class="sm">1.1 {'{B}'} Συμεών א A …</text>
        <text x="242" y="176" class="sm">// Σίμων 𝔓72 B Ψ …</text>
        <text x="242" y="204" class="lbl">apparatus popup</text>
        <rect x="436" y="32" width="190" height="254" rx="6" class="pane"/>
        <text x="448" y="52" class="h">Text Collection</text>
        <text x="448" y="78" class="sm">Greek</text><rect x="500" y="72" width="110" height="6" class="ln"/>
        <text x="448" y="98" class="sm">TR</text><rect x="500" y="92" width="100" height="6" class="ln"/>
        <text x="448" y="118" class="sm">Hindi</text><rect x="500" y="112" width="110" height="6" class="ln"/>
        <text x="448" y="138" class="sm">Tamil</text><rect x="500" y="132" width="90" height="6" class="ln"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption>A schematic, not a screenshot: one way to arrange the windows.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The Source Language Text</h2>
  <p>A Source Language Text shows the Hebrew or Greek text with help for each word: point at a word and the status bar gives its dictionary form (<em>lemma</em>), its grammatical form and an English gloss. Paratext offers three pairings, each with one Old Testament text and one New Testament text:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ptx-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Choose</th><th>Old Testament</th><th>New Testament</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>HEB/GRK</strong></td><td>Biblia Hebraica Stuttgartensia (Hebrew)</td><td>UBS Greek New Testament, 5th edition</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LXX/GRK</strong></td><td>Septuaginta 2006 (Greek)</td><td>UBS Greek New Testament, 5th edition</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>OHEB/OGRK</strong></td><td>Open Scriptures Hebrew Bible 2.1</td><td>UBS Greek New Testament, 4th edition</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>For the UBS5 apparatus, choose <strong>HEB/GRK</strong> or <strong>LXX/GRK</strong>. You can switch between the three in the window's toolbar.</p>

  <h2>Opening it</h2>
  <ol class="ptx-steps">
    <li>Click the main menu icon and choose <em>Paratext &gt; Open</em>.</li>
    <li>Click the <em>Source Language Texts</em> button.</li>
    <li>Choose HEB/GRK, LXX/GRK or OHEB/OGRK.</li>
    <li>Under <em>Open As</em>, choose Floating, Tab, Panel or Autohide, and click <em>OK</em>.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="ptx-callout">
    <p class="ptx-k">Cannot open it?</p>
    <p>Access comes through your organisation. If you did not name your organisation when you registered with Paratext, add it to your account at registry.paratext.org, or ask your Paratext support person.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Installing or updating the texts</h2>
  <ol class="ptx-steps">
    <li>Open <em>Download/Install resources</em> and choose <em>Internet (DBL and Paratext servers)</em>.</li>
    <li>Click the <em>Source Language Texts</em> button.</li>
    <li>Tick the texts you need and click <em>Download/Install</em>. An <em>Update</em> label shows that a newer version is waiting.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The Source Language Texts tab lists these:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ptx-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Text</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>GRK</code></td><td>UBS Greek New Testament, 5th edition</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>OGRK</code></td><td>UBS Greek New Testament, 4th edition</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>BYZ</code></td><td>Robinson-Pierpont Byzantine New Testament: the Byzantine (Majority) text. It is close to the <a href={url('glossary/#tr')}>Textus Receptus</a> but not the same.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>HEB</code></td><td>Biblia Hebraica Stuttgartensia</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>OHEB</code></td><td>Open Scriptures Hebrew Bible 2.1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>LXX</code>, <code>LXA</code></td><td>Septuaginta 2006, and an alternative Septuagint text for some books</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Each text is licensed to you or your organisation through the Digital Bible Library (DBL), so a colleague may see a text that you cannot.</p>

  <h2>Interlinear lines</h2>
  <p>Under each Hebrew or Greek word the window can show a transliteration, the lemma, the form and a gloss. Turn these on from the window's tab menu, under <em>View</em>. The glosses that come with Paratext are in English.</p>

  <h2>Other helpful resources</h2>
  <p>These are ordinary resources, installed from the <em>Resources</em> button of the same dialog:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ptx-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Search for</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>NET08</td><td><code>net</code></td><td>The NET Bible notes. Text-critical notes are their own note type, separate from translators' notes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TNN (and TND, if offered)</td><td><code>notes</code></td><td>Translator's Notes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HBKENG</td><td><code>handbook</code></td><td>The UBS Handbook Series in English.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Some resources stop part way through a book. If one has nothing for your verse, that is normal. You did not set it up wrongly.</p>

  <h2>Arranging the windows</h2>
  <p>Put the Source Language Text in its own window beside your project. To compare several texts at one verse, use a <em>Text Collection</em>: it shows the same verse from several projects and resources at once, and scrolls along with your other windows.</p>
  <ol class="ptx-steps">
    <li>From the main menu choose <em>Open text collection</em>.</li>
    <li>Add the texts you want to compare, for example the major-language Bibles your readers know (Hindi or Tamil).</li>
    <li>The simple one-pane view hides footnotes and their marks. From the collection's tab menu choose <em>View &gt; Two panes</em>.</li>
    <li>Click the name of a text. The right pane shows it with more context.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What the apparatus looks like</h2>
  <p>The Source Language Text marks apparatus entries with small red characters. Click one and a popup shows the entry.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ptx-table marks">
      <thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Where</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="red">1–9</td><td>after a Greek word</td><td>an entry in the UBS textual apparatus</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="red">a–z</td><td>after a Greek verse</td><td>an entry in the discourse segmentation apparatus</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="red">♦</td><td>after a Greek word</td><td>two readings are equally good; see <a href={url('glossary/#diamond')}>♦</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="red">*</td><td>start of a Greek verse</td><td>a cross-reference entry</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="red">t</td><td>after a Hebrew word</td><td>a textual issue in the Hebrew Old Testament Text Project (HOTTP) report</td></tr>
        <tr><td>* / **</td><td>before a Hebrew word</td><td><em>ketiv</em> (the written form) / <em>qere</em> (the form to be read)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The popup ends with a <em>More help…</em> link. At 2 Peter 1:1 the manuscripts differ on the spelling of Peter's other name:</p>
  <figure class="ptx-specimen">
    <pre class="app"><span lang="grc">1.1 {'{B}'} Συμεών</span> <span class="siglum" lang="he" dir="ltr">א</span> A 33 307 436 442 642 1175 1448 1735 1739 1852 2344 Byz<sup>pt</sup> [K L P] Lect arm geo // <span lang="grc">Σίμων</span> 𝔓<sup>72</sup> B Ψ 5 81 1243 1611 2492 Byz<sup>pt</sup> ℓ 147 ℓ 590 … it<sup>ar,z</sup> vg cop<sup>sa,bo</sup> eth slav BTI DHH?</pre>
    <figcaption>UBS5 apparatus at 2 Peter 1:1, as Paratext shows it</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>The sigla (short names for witnesses) are printed just as in the UBS edition. Paratext does not expand them when you point at them. Here is how to read this entry:</p>
  <ul class="ptx-read">
    <li><a href={url('glossary/#rating')}>{'{B}'}</a> is the committee's rating for the reading printed in the text.</li>
    <li>The first reading, <span lang="grc">Συμεών</span>, comes with the witnesses that support it.</li>
    <li><a href={url('glossary/#double-slash')}>//</a> starts the next reading, <span lang="grc">Σίμων</span>, and its witnesses.</li>
    <li><a href={url('glossary/#byz')}>Byz</a><sup>pt</sup> turns up on both sides. Part of the Byzantine manuscripts supports each reading; they do not speak with one voice.</li>
    <li>"DHH?" is a modern translation cited with a question mark. It probably supports the reading, but its wording leaves some doubt.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a full walk through one entry, see <a href={url('guide/apparatus/')}>How to read a UBS apparatus entry</a>.</p>

  <h2>Decoding the sigla</h2>
  <p>Paratext Help has the full UBS5 list of symbols and abbreviations. Search Help for "What is the meaning of the abbreviations used in the apparatuses?". It tells you what a symbol means, such as <code>{'{ }'}</code> or <code>[ ]</code>. It does not tell you what a manuscript is or how old it is.</p>
  <p>For that, use this site. The <a href={url('')}>lookup page</a> takes a siglum and opens the witness. Short addresses work too: <a href={url('p66/')}>{url('p66/')}</a> opens 𝔓⁶⁶, and <a href={url('aleph/')}>{url('aleph/')}</a> opens Codex Sinaiticus (<span class="siglum" lang="he" dir="ltr">א</span>). Each witness page gives the date, the contents and where the manuscript is kept.</p>

  <h2>If you cannot get the resources</h2>
  <ul class="ptx-alt">
    <li>The NET Bible notes, text-critical notes included, are free at <a href="https://netbible.org">netbible.org</a>.</li>
    <li>The INTF in Münster lists the Greek manuscripts, with dates and many images, in the New Testament Virtual Manuscript Room: <a href="https://ntvmr.uni-muenster.de">ntvmr.uni-muenster.de</a>.</li>
    <li>You can read Codex Sinaiticus page by page at <a href="https://codexsinaiticus.org">codexsinaiticus.org</a>, and Codex Vaticanus at <a href="https://digi.vatlib.it">digi.vatlib.it</a>.</li>
    <li>The Textus Receptus editions are in the public domain, so you can find free copies and load them.</li>
  </ul>

  <aside class="unverified" aria-labelledby="unv">
    <h2 id="unv">Not yet verified</h2>
    <p>These points are not confirmed by Paratext Help or by a screen we have seen. Check them in your own copy of Paratext.</p>
    <ul>
      
      <li>Where the <em>More help…</em> link in the apparatus popup leads.</li>
      <li>Whether the UBS apparatus popup works inside a Text Collection, or only in the resource's own window.</li>
    </ul>
  </aside>
  
</Context>

<style>
